**Objective:** This study aims to develop and evaluate a free-breathing T2-weighted turbo spin echo (TSE) sequence in 4D (3D plus respiration), incorporating motion compensation, for applications in radiology and MR-guided radiotherapy.

**Approach:** Continuous acquisition of k-space data was conducted using rCASPR—a technique combining rewounded Cartesian acquisition with spiral profiling to match the contrast of standard linear phase encoding while organizing data into multiple respiratory phases. Low-respiration-corrected 4D images were reconstructed using compressed sensing to derive non-rigid deformation vector fields; these were then utilized for motion-compensated final reconstruction. Performance of rCASPR sampling, linear sampling, and conventional Cartesian sampling (refraction) with spiral profiles was compared regarding point-spread function (PSF) and image contrast through in silico simulations, phantom studies, and animal experiments. A systematic parameters optimization was employed to determine ideal settings for low-resolution 4D-MRI in terms of grid search. Validation of motion compensation and rCASPR sampling included testing across eight healthy individuals against free-breathing linear-sacanned sequences. Evaluation metrics included visual subjective assessments, inter-subject agreement, and quantitative analysis of gradient entropy.

